unny lines.  \n   Among all non‑sunny lines the most “efficient’’ ones are the vertical lines\n   \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots\\); they are pairwise disjoint and each of them contains the\n   maximal possible number of new points.  \n   If we take the first \\(m\\) vertical lines, the number of distinct points they\n   cover is  \n\n   \\[\n   f(m)=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}(n+1-i)=m(n+1)-\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   No other choice of \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines can cover more than \\(f(m)\\) points,\n   because any horizontal or diagonal line would intersect each already chosen\n   vertical line in a point that is already counted, and therefore could not\n   increase the total number of *new* points.  \n   Consequently we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   N-2k\\le f(n-k).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(N=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) and the expression (1) for \\(f\\) into (2)\n   and simplifying gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k^{2}-3k\\le 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad 0\\le k\\le 3 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **no configuration can contain more than three sunny lines**.\n\n4. **Realising the four possibilities.**  \n   We now exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n   covers all required points and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for each\n   \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   All of them are non‑sunny and together they contain every lattice point of the\n   triangle.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n   All points are covered except \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Add the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{1}:\\;y=2x-(2n-1)\n   \\]\n\n   which passes through \\((n,1)\\) and is not parallel to any forbidden direction.\n   Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\).  \n   The uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{1}=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_{2}=(n-1,2),\\qqua