se the remaining \\(k-1\\) sunny lines\n   \\[\n   L_j:\\;y = \\frac{1}{j}\\,x+\\Bigl(1-\\frac{1}{j}\\Bigr)\\qquad (j=2,\\dots ,k) .\n   \\]\n   Each line \\(L_j\\) contains the point \\((t+j,1)\\) and also the point \\((1,j)\\) (already covered by a vertical line); consequently it covers the whole column \\(C_j\\).  The \\(k\\) sunny lines are distinct, and together with the \\(t\\) vertical lines they cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Thus for every \\(k\\in\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Assume that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists; let the other \\(n-2\\) lines be non‑sunny.  \n   Replace every non‑sunny line by a vertical line that covers the same set of points (this never increases the number of uncovered points).  Hence we may assume that the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines are vertical and cover \\(n-2\\) distinct columns.  \n   The two remaining columns are the right‑most ones, namely\n   \\[\n   (n-1,1),(n-1,2),\\qquad (n,1).\n   \\]\n   Any line through two of these three points has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line can contain at most one of them.  Because we have only two sunny lines, at most two of the three points can be covered – a contradiction.  Hence no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n4. **Collecting the results.**  \n   * For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable (constructions above).  \n   * For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values \\(k=3,4,\\dots ,n\\) are attainable (construction for \\(k\\ge 3\\)).  \n   * The value \\(k=2\\) is never attainable (impossibility proof).  \n\n   Therefore the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the verdict.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is *non‑sunny*.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(x=a\\); hence all points are covered and no line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nPut \\(t=n-1\\).  Use the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t .\n\\]\n\nThe only points of \\(T_n\\) not lying on one of these lines are the two points\n\\((t,1)\\) and \\((1,t)\\).  They are joined by the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=\\frac{1}{t}\\,x+\\Bigl(1-\\frac{1}{t}\\Bigr) .\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(\\frac1t\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(L\\) is sunny.\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) are now covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.3  \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nt=n-k\\qquad (0\\le t\\le n-3).\n\\]\n\nTake the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe columns that are still uncovered are  \n\n\\[\nC_j=\\{(t+j,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le k+1-j\\},\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\((t+j,j)\\;(j=1,\\dots ,k)\\) lie on the line  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;y=x-t .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(D\\) equals \\(1\\); consequently \\(D\\) is sunny and it covers the\n“upper’’ point of every column \\(C_j\\).\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\;y=\\frac{1}{j}\\,x+\\Bigl(1-\\frac{1}{j}\\Bigr) .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_j\\) passes through the points \\((t+j,1)\\) and \\((1,j)\\); the latter\nbelongs to a column already covered by (1).  Hence \\(L_j\\) covers the whole\ncolumn \\(C_j\\).  Its slope \\(\\frac1j\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so\n\\(L_j\\) is sunny.  The lines \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct because they have\ndifferent slopes.\n\nTogether with \\(D\\) we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines; together with the\n\\(t\\) vertical lines (1) we have \\(t+k=n\\) distinct lines, and every point of\n\\(T_n\\) belongs to one of them.  Thus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines exists for every \\(k\\in\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of two sunny lines\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and\ncontains exactly two sunny lines.  Let the other \\(n-2\\) lines be non‑sunny.\n\n*Step 1 – reduction to vertical lines.*  \nIf a non‑sunny line is horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\), replace it by a vertical\nline that passes through the same set of points of \\(T_n\\); this can only\nincrease the set of points covered by the non‑sunny lines.  Consequently we may\nassume that the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines are vertical and that they are the\nlines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus the only columns not completely covered are the two right‑most ones,\nnamely the points\n\n\\[\nP_1=(n-1,1),\\;P_2=(n-1,2),\\;P_3=(n,1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Step 2 – the two sunny lines cannot cover the three points.*  \nAny line through two of the points in (6) has slope\n\n* \\(0\\)   for the pair \\((n-1,1),(n-1,2)\\) (vertical),\n* \\(\\infty\\) for the pair \\((n,1),(n-1,1)\\) (horizontal),\n* \\(-1\\) for the pair \\((n,1),(n-1,2)\\).\n\nAll three slopes are forbidden for a sunny line.  Hence a sunny line can contain\nat most one of the points in (6).  With only two sunny lines we can cover at\nmost two of the three points, contradicting the requirement that every point of\n\\(T_n\\) be covered.\n\nTherefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for\nany \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the classification\n\nFrom Sections 1 and 2 we have:\n\n* \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always attainable.\n* Every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable (construction of\n  Section 1.3).\n* \\(k=2\\) is never attainable (Section 2).\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\) reduces to \\(\\{3\\}\\); consequently the\nfeasible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).  For every \\(n\\ge 4\\) the feasible values are\n\\(\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\), i.e. all non‑negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\)\nexcept \\(2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\;}\n\\]\n\nIn words: for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) all non‑negative integers \\(k\\le n\\) are\nrealizable except \\(k=2\\); for \\(n=3\\) this description yields the set\n\\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
