            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions for the attainable values\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(x=a\\); hence all points are covered and no line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nPut \\(t=n-1\\).  Use the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t .\n\\]\n\nThe only points of \\(T_n\\) not lying on one of these lines are \\((t,1)\\) and \\((1,t)\\).  \nThe line through them is  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=\\frac1t\\,x+\\Bigl(1-\\frac1t\\Bigr),\n\\]\n\nwhose slope \\(\\frac1t\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(L\\) is sunny.  All points of \\(T_n\\) are now covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.3  \\(k=3\\)\n\nTake the two vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad x=2\n\\]\n\n(which are non‑sunny).  Consider the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1&:\\;y=x-1,\\\\\nL_2&:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\\\\nL_3&:\\;y=-\\tfrac13x+\\tfrac43 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA direct check shows that every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies at least one of the five equations\n\\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=x-1,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\;y=-\\tfrac13x+\\tfrac43\\); consequently the three lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) are sunny and together with the two vertical lines they cover all of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of two sunny lines\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly two sunny lines.  Let the other \\(n-2\\) lines be non‑sunny.\n\n*Step 1 – reduction to vertical lines.*  \nIf a non‑sunny line is horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\), replace it by a vertical line that passes through the same points of \\(T_n\\); this replacement cannot decrease the set of covered points.  Hence we may assume that the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2 .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n*Step 2 – the uncovered points.*  \nThe only points of \\(T_n\\) not lying on the lines (2.1) are  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,2),\\qquad P_3=(n,1).\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n*Step 3 – a sunny line can contain at most one of the points (2.2).*  \nThe slopes of the three possible pairs are  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{slope}(P_1,P_2)=\\infty,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{slope}(P_1,P_3)=0,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{slope}(P_2,P_3)=-1,\n\\]\n\nall of which are forbidden for a sunny line.  Hence each sunny line can contain at most one of the points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\).\n\n*Step 4 – contradiction.*  \nWith only two sunny lines we can cover at most two of the three points in (2.2), contradicting the requirement that every point of \\(T_n\\) be covered.  Therefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of four or more sunny lines\n\nAssume a configuration with at least four sunny lines exists.  As before we may replace all non‑sunny lines by vertical ones, obtaining a family consisting of some vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\) (with \\(m\\le n-4\\)) and at least four sunny lines.\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) that are not on the vertical lines are precisely the points belonging to the three right‑most columns:\n\n\\[\nQ_1=(n-2,1),\\;Q_2=(n-2,2),\\;\nQ_3=(n-1,1),\\;Q_4=(n-1,2),\\;\nQ_5=(n,1).\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nAny two of the three points \\(Q_2,Q_4,Q_5\\) determine a forbidden slope (\\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)); consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of these three points.  Since we have at least four sunny lines, at most four of the five points in (3.1) can be covered, leaving at least one point uncovered – a contradiction.  Hence no configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the classification\n\nFrom Sections 1–3 we have:\n\n* \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always attainable.\n* \\(k=3\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n* \\(k=2\\) and every \\(k\\ge4\\) are impossible.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor all integers \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
